WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.520
Most inhibitory neurons of the mouse cortex are not born in the cortex.

2
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.660
The excitatory neurons are.

3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.000
They are made in the wall of the cortex, and climb straight out to their layers.

4
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.900
So where do the inhibitory neurons come from?

5
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:22.513
They start lower, in the ventral forebrain, in swellings called the ganglionic

6
00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:27.033
eminences. These are temporary zones packed with dividing progenitors.

7
00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:32.773
In cultured rat embryos, dye placed there labels some cells that run sideways into

8
00:00:32.773 --> 00:00:39.713
the cortex. In mouse embryos lacking both Dlx1 and Dlx2, the cells make no such

9
00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:43.073
trip. The cortex ends up with few inhibitory neurons.

10
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:48.713
A pyramidal neuron moves at right angles to the surface, mostly by gripping a radial

11
00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:54.293
glial fibre. An interneuron moves parallel to the surface, with no glial fibre to

12
00:00:54.293 --> 00:00:59.193
follow. Early on, in the cortex, interneurons travel in two streams.

13
00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:01.653
One runs just beneath the meninges.

14
00:01:02.293 --> 00:01:03.773
The other runs deep in the wall.

15
00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:08.213
Between them, the young cortical plate holds few interneurons early on.

16
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:12.903
Why do cells stay in the streams, instead of diving into the plate at once?

17
00:01:13.523 --> 00:01:18.083
Chemokines are small secreted proteins, first known for steering immune cells.

18
00:01:18.563 --> 00:01:23.423
One chemokine, called CXCL12, lines both streams.

19
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:27.643
The meninges make it, and so do dividing precursors in the deep wall.

20
00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:32.443
The interneurons carry its receptor, CXCR4.

21
00:01:32.943 --> 00:01:37.663
In a chamber assay, striatal precursor cells moved toward it, but not when it was

22
00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:39.463
added equally on both sides.

23
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:43.017
Take away the receptor, and the cells leave their streams.

24
00:01:43.637 --> 00:01:47.957
They enter the cortical plate early, and they spread less widely across the cortex.

25
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:51.877
The chemokine controls retention, timing and spread.

26
00:01:52.477 --> 00:01:57.197
Normally, a cell seems to stop answering CXCL12 to leave a stream.

27
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.817
Then it turns into the plate, toward its layer.

28
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.920
So the long way round is a series of decisions.

29
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.940
In mice, most interneurons are born outside the cortex, and travel sideways.

30
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.420
A chemokine keeps them in their lanes.

31
00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.180
Keep this rule. Cells seem to stay on the route until they stop answering the

32
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:15.500
signal.
